INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Humming dryers. Yellow light. A row of plastic chairs bolted to the floor.

YUSUF, 50s, folds shirts. GRETA, 40s, stares at a spinning drum. LAMAR, 20s, sleeps with his head against a machine. WANJIRU, 30s, carries a basket and a toddler's sock, looking for the toddler. And BARTHOLOMEW, 70s, sits at the end of the row reading a seed catalogue he has read forty times.

Yusuf squares a sleeve. Greta stands, opens her dryer, pulls out a handful of warm towels, sniffs them, and stuffs them back. Wanjiru lifts the toddler's sock to the light, sets it on a machine, and moves to the next row while Lamar shifts in his sleep and Bartholomew turns a page.

YUSUF
(not looking up)
Greta, your towels are done.

GRETA
I know.

YUSUF
You're not taking them out.

GRETA
Not yet.

WANJIRU
Has anyone seen a little boy? Blue shirt, no left shoe?

YUSUF
Sorry.

GRETA
No.

BARTHOLOMEW
(without looking up)
Under the folding table.

Wanjiru bends. Under the table, a small boy lies on his stomach, asleep, one shoe off. She lifts him. He doesn't wake. She sets the sock on his foot, balances him on her hip, and carries the basket with her other hand.

WANJIRU
Thank you.

BARTHOLOMEW
Tomatoes this year.

Wanjiru nods at nothing and goes out. The door chimes. The dryers keep humming.
